1
00:00:00,801 --> 00:00:03,289
It's two in the morning, and checkout is failing.

2
00:00:03,289 --> 00:00:06,336
In ten minutes, three changes went live. Which one broke it?

3
00:00:06,336 --> 00:00:08,918
Roll back the wrong one, and it gets worse.

4
00:00:08,918 --> 00:00:10,913
The answer lives in the connections.

5
00:00:10,913 --> 00:00:15,862
Dashboards show symptoms, and today's AI agents answer with confidence, not proof.

6
00:00:15,862 --> 00:00:19,223
What if an agent could only blame what it can prove as a path?

7
00:00:19,223 --> 00:00:24,239
That's Faultline. No path, no claim. And the engine that makes it possible is FalkorDB.

8
00:00:24,239 --> 00:00:30,633
This cube is a real system's graph. Open it: services, deploys, people, and the proof path between them.

9
00:00:30,633 --> 00:00:33,190
Inside FalkorDB, alerts, deploys, lineage,

10
00:00:33,190 --> 00:00:38,662
docs and people stream into one graph per customer, each with its own read-only user.

11
00:00:38,662 --> 00:00:48,540
Every relationship is a sparse matrix, walked with linear algebra: 296 thousand nodes and 1.1 million edges fit in 490 megabytes.

12
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:55,598
Checkout alerts. A breadth-first search spreads out inside the database: the blast radius, in a fraction of a millisecond.

13
00:00:55,598 --> 00:01:01,219
Three deploys landed. For the recommendation deploy, the shortest-path procedure returns zero rows.

14
00:01:01,219 --> 00:01:03,338
No path, so it cannot be blamed.

15
00:01:03,338 --> 00:01:09,371
But a path is not enough. Max flow shows the email deploy can carry only 2 percent of checkout's traffic.

16
00:01:09,371 --> 00:01:10,876
Rejected, with proof.

17
00:01:10,876 --> 00:01:17,442
The minimum cut is the smallest containment, tried first on a graph copy made in 383 milliseconds.

18
00:01:17,442 --> 00:01:22,263
Centrality ranks who should fix it, and a spanning forest draws the escalation chain.

19
00:01:22,263 --> 00:01:28,236
The model can only point: every read is read-only, and one atomic write records a human's approval.

20
00:01:28,236 --> 00:01:34,649
All 10 graph algorithms change a decision. Without max flow, the right cause drops from 20 of 20 to 0.

21
00:01:34,649 --> 00:01:39,864
Switch FalkorDB off, and Faultline confirms nothing. FalkorDB is the brain.

22
00:01:39,864 --> 00:01:47,413
Now, live. The incident opens, and a crew of agents gets to work, calling the graph's tools over M.C.P.: recall, scope, suspects.

23
00:01:47,413 --> 00:01:49,468
Every step lands in the graph.

24
00:01:49,468 --> 00:01:54,291
The gate rejects the recommendation deploy: the exact Cypher, and its zero rows.

25
00:01:54,291 --> 00:01:58,132
The email deploy's path is too narrow: max flow rejects it.

26
00:01:58,132 --> 00:02:03,328
The payment deploy passes. Here's the proof path, and every piece of evidence behind it.

27
00:02:04,201 --> 00:02:08,046
The smallest containment, tested on a copy, and who should fix it.

28
00:02:08,046 --> 00:02:14,357
A human approves the exact change, bound to its plan hash. Faultline opens a real pull request, merges it, and verifies it.

29
00:02:14,357 --> 00:02:19,181
Applied. Then it writes the decision back into FalkorDB. That pull request is real, on GitHub,

30
00:02:19,181 --> 00:02:30,300
with the plan hash in its body; it counts only once GitHub's main has the change and checkout is healthy again. Meanwhile the on-call has been paged: in the app, by email, on Telegram or through PagerDuty.

31
00:02:38,847 --> 00:02:42,527
Faultline pages whoever the graph says is on call for the owning team.

32
00:02:42,527 --> 00:02:46,386
On their phone: the verdict, the proof path, and who fixes it.

33
00:02:47,641 --> 00:02:50,937
One tap acknowledges it, and the escalation stops.

34
00:02:50,937 --> 00:03:01,167
After a verified fix, the on-call can grant a standing approval: one use, seven days. When the same fault comes back, the gate still proves the cause, and a second real pull request merges and verifies.

35
00:03:01,167 --> 00:03:06,636
Handled while you slept. Zero people woken. That second incident came in through the API, like a real alert.

36
00:03:06,636 --> 00:03:13,616
The contract is now spent, so the next repeat pages a person again, and the on-call gets only a quiet notice, never a wake-up.

37
00:03:21,784 --> 00:03:29,947
Another kind of incident: the executive revenue KPI jumps. The proof path runs six hops, from a payment cutover through the data lineage to the KPI.

38
00:03:29,947 --> 00:03:35,572
Faultline measures the data itself: one segment, a power of ten off, and proposes the one-line repair.

39
00:03:35,572 --> 00:03:41,963
A human approves the exact diff. The warehouse is rebuilt, 32 invariants pass. Applied, and written back.

40
00:03:45,933 --> 00:03:49,138
It's the team's memory, too. Who decided the payment timeout?

41
00:03:49,138 --> 00:03:51,700
Every name in the answer comes from a query.

42
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:56,546
And it learns from the people using it: the fixes your team prefers and the guidelines they set.

43
00:03:56,546 --> 00:04:00,329
The next plan puts your team's choice first: moved up from number two.

44
00:04:00,329 --> 00:04:06,310
And FalkorDB shows its own work: the memory each graph takes, every query's timing, and the live tail.

45
00:04:06,310 --> 00:04:11,142
Now break it yourself: remove one call from a copy of the graph, and watch the verdict move.

46
00:04:11,142 --> 00:04:12,758
The graph is load-bearing.

47
00:04:12,758 --> 00:04:20,526
The numbers. On 140 worlds built to fool it: 0 confident wrong blames, the right cause in 51 of 100, and not sure on the rest.

48
00:04:20,526 --> 00:04:30,821
A strong language model blamed an innocent change 37 times. On a real system, the OpenTelemetry Demo: the right cause in 11 of 20 faults, 0 wrong blames in 40 windows.

49
00:04:30,821 --> 00:04:33,543
A telemetry threshold was wrong 8 times.

50
00:04:33,543 --> 00:04:38,823
Refusing is cheap: no path in 0.053 milliseconds and zero tokens.

51
00:04:38,823 --> 00:04:42,652
And it scales: 250 customer graphs, 0 leaks.

52
00:04:42,652 --> 00:04:45,489
It's live now: 5 of 5 real runs, verified.

53
00:04:45,489 --> 00:04:50,489
Any agent can use the same tools over M.C.P. Faultline, built on FalkorDB.

54
00:04:50,489 --> 00:04:52,306
No path, no claim.

